Never call t.Fatal, t.Fatalf, t.FailNow, or any function that invokes runtime.Goexit inside a goroutine spawned by the code under test. These calls terminate only the spawned goroutine, not the test goroutine. If the goroutine is expected to send a result on a channel, the collection loop in the test will deadlock waiting forever — the test hangs instead of failing with a clear message.

Correct pattern:
- Use t.Errorf (non-fatal) inside the goroutine to record the failure
- Use a flag variable (sync/atomic bool or a channel) to signal unexpected invocations back to the test goroutine
- Ensure the goroutine always completes normally (sends to any expected channel) even after recording a failure

This rule applies to any goroutine whose lifecycle is not fully controlled by the test — goroutines spawned internally by the function under test, or goroutines passed as callbacks.

Example anti-pattern:
  // WRONG: t.Fatal in goroutine causes deadlock if the goroutine must send on resultsChan
  type neverCalledInvoker struct { t *testing.T }
  func (n *neverCalledInvoker) Invoke(...) { n.t.Fatal("should not be called") }

Example correct pattern:
  var invoked int32
  type neverCalledInvoker struct{}
  func (n *neverCalledInvoker) Invoke(...) (llmadapter.InvocationResult, error) {
      atomic.StoreInt32(&invoked, 1)
      return llmadapter.InvocationResult{}, nil  // still completes normally
  }
  // After the function under test returns:
  if atomic.LoadInt32(&invoked) != 0 { t.Error("invoker should not have been called") }

When spawning N goroutines each expected to send exactly once to a channel, that channel must be buffered to at least N. An unbuffered channel is safe only for 1:1 synchronization (one sender, one receiver, both running concurrently). With N senders and one collector:

- If any goroutine panics or exits without sending, the collector loop blocks forever waiting for a send that never arrives — goroutine leak and deadlock.
- There is no recover path in the collector; the only safe option is to ensure every goroutine always sends before returning.

Correct pattern:
  ch := make(chan result, len(items))
  for i, item := range items {
      go func(idx int, it T) {
          defer func() {
              if r := recover(); r != nil {
                  ch <- result{index: idx, failSafe: true}
              }
          }()
          ch <- result{index: idx, value: compute(it)}
      }(i, item)
  }
  for range items {
      r := <-ch
      // process r
  }

Alternative: use sync.WaitGroup with a slice + mutex if ordering must be preserved without index-keyed results.

This rule applies to any fan-out pattern where goroutine count is determined at runtime — not just fixed-N cases.

When a result struct maintains both a complete collection (e.g., AllFindings) and one or more filtered subsets (e.g., BlockingFindings), any mutation that changes a finding's fields (severity downgrade, status change) must update the finding in ALL collections that contain it.

This commonly arises when a post-processing step (like a verifier) modifies items already placed into the result struct. If only the filtered subset is updated, the complete collection retains the stale pre-mutation value. Downstream code that reads the complete collection — such as event serialization, findingsBySeverity maps, or display rendering — will produce contradictory output.

Anti-pattern (only BlockingFindings updated):
  result.BlockingFindings = filteredKept  // correct: downgraded findings removed
  // result.AllFindings still has the Error-severity copy of the downgraded finding
  // ReviewResultEvent will emit: blocking_findings=0, findings_by_severity.error=1

Correct pattern:
  result.BlockingFindings = filteredKept
  // Also update AllFindings to reflect the new severity
  for i, f := range result.AllFindings {
      if downgradedFiles[f.File+":"+strconv.Itoa(f.Line)] {
          result.AllFindings[i].Severity = SeverityWarning
      }
  }

This rule applies to any code that mutates finding-like objects after they have been distributed into multiple slices. The invariant to maintain: AllFindings is always consistent with BlockingFindings — no item can appear with Severity=Error in AllFindings while being absent from BlockingFindings.
